WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.902
Task statement 1.3.

2
00:00:01.902 --> 00:00:06.404
Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning.

3
00:00:06.404 --> 00:00:16.735
A synthesis subagent that never saw the search results will invent or drop citations, so what you pass at spawn time decides report quality.

4
00:00:18.135 --> 00:00:20.949
Spawning a subagent is just another tool call.

5
00:00:20.949 --> 00:00:26.577
The coordinator calls the Task tool with a prompt, and that prompt is all the subagent gets.

6
00:00:26.577 --> 00:00:32.022
It did not see the earlier conversation, the files you read, or the results you gathered.

7
00:00:32.022 --> 00:00:35.754
Treat it like briefing a contractor who missed every meeting.

8
00:00:35.754 --> 00:00:38.017
If it matters, write it in the brief.

9
00:00:39.417 --> 00:00:44.593
In the guide's terms, you list Task in allowed tools so the coordinator can spawn helpers.

10
00:00:44.593 --> 00:00:49.711
Current docs treat allowed tools as pre-approval, but the exam keeps the guide's wording.

11
00:00:49.711 --> 00:00:54.886
Each helper gets an agent definition with a description, a prompt and its own tool limits.

12
00:00:54.886 --> 00:01:00.062
The description matters most, because the coordinator reads it to decide when to delegate.

13
00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:03.972
Current docs call this tool Agent, and Task still works as an alias.

14
00:01:05.372 --> 00:01:09.143
Because nothing is inherited, the prompt must carry the facts.

15
00:01:09.143 --> 00:01:16.988
Pass findings as structured data where each claim keeps its own source title, link and date, instead of blending them into prose.

16
00:01:16.988 --> 00:01:21.549
To run subagents in parallel, emit several Task calls in a single response.

17
00:01:21.549 --> 00:01:26.961
And describe the goal and the quality bar, then let the subagent decide how to get there.

18
00:01:28.361 --> 00:01:32.011
Back in the research system, the four searches have finished.

19
00:01:32.011 --> 00:01:38.471
The coordinator now briefs the synthesis subagent with a structured list of claims, each tied to its source.

20
00:01:38.471 --> 00:01:43.138
The brief states the goal and the bar: cover every scope and cite every claim.

21
00:01:43.138 --> 00:01:48.223
Two independent fact checks are launched together, as two Task calls in one response.

22
00:01:49.623 --> 00:01:53.802
The most common trap is assuming the subagent already knows things.

23
00:01:53.802 --> 00:01:54.551
It does not.

24
00:01:54.551 --> 00:02:03.285
Also watch for setups that forget Task in allowed tools, that spread parallel work across several turns, or that flatten sources into prose.

25
00:02:03.285 --> 00:02:11.020
On the exam, the right answer keeps context explicit and structured, and launches independent work together in one response.

26
00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:12.986
To recap.

27
00:02:12.986 --> 00:02:16.381
The Task tool spawns subagents, so it must be allowed.

28
00:02:16.381 --> 00:02:20.656
Nothing carries over from the parent, so the brief must be complete.

29
00:02:20.656 --> 00:02:24.491
Keep source details in their own fields so citations survive.

30
00:02:24.491 --> 00:02:31.407
And when work is independent, send several Task calls in one response, each with a clear goal and quality bar.

